# C / C++ patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
C/C++ ownership and resource management (C++ Core Guidelines). Applies to
both C and C++ files.

## RAII and ownership (C++)

- Every resource (heap memory, file handle, mutex lock, socket) is owned by
  exactly one object whose destructor releases it; no hand-written `new`/
  `delete` pair. Use a smart pointer, `std::lock_guard`/`std::unique_lock`,
  or another RAII type.
- Rule of Zero by default; when a class must own a resource directly, Rule
  of Five (define all five special members, or `= delete` the unwanted
  ones).
- Hand a resource back by return value, not by an out-parameter raw pointer.
- Never return a pointer or reference to a local variable or a member of a
  temporary.

## Manual resource management (C)

- Pair every `malloc`/`fopen`/`pthread_mutex_lock`-style acquire with its
  release on every exit path, error paths included; use a `goto cleanup;`
  single exit-and-release block.
- A function that allocates and returns a pointer documents in its header
  comment who owns the result and how to release it.
- Never `free` a pointer twice; set a pointer to `NULL` right after freeing
  it when it could be read or freed again on a later path.

## Containers and iterators

- Do not hold an iterator, pointer, or reference into a `std::vector` across
  `push_back` past capacity, `insert`, or `erase`; re-fetch it or use the
  iterator `erase`/`insert` return.
- `std::vector`/`std::array`/`std::string` over a raw owning array or
  hand-rolled dynamic buffer.

## Composition and interfaces

- Composition over deep inheritance; polymorphism only when callers must
  treat different concrete types uniformly.
- A polymorphic C++ base class has a `virtual` destructor.
- A public C API keeps its surface small with ownership rules explicit in the
  header; prefer an opaque handle plus accessors over exposing a struct's
  layout.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A "god header" (`utils.h`, `common.h`).
- C-style casts (`(Type)x`) in C++; use `static_cast`/`dynamic_cast`/
  `const_cast`/`reinterpret_cast`.
- Output parameters faking multiple return values where `std::tuple`/
  `std::pair`/a small struct (in C, a returned struct) fits.
